There appears to be a redundant conditional check in the `solve` method. The code first checks `if (lhs.IsProduct() && lhs.FindVa())` and then immediately checks `if (lhs.IsProduct())` inside that block. Since we already know `lhs.IsProduct()` is true from the outer condition, this inner check is unnecessary and could be simplified.

```cpp
// Current code:
if (lhs.IsProduct() && lhs.FindVa()) {
    Valuable coefficient = constants::one;
    Valuable radical;

    // Extract coefficient and radical from the product
    if (lhs.IsProduct()) { // <-- This check is redundant
        auto& product = lhs.as<Product>();
        // ...
    }
}

// Suggested simplification:
if (lhs.IsProduct() && lhs.FindVa()) {
    Valuable coefficient = constants::one;
    Valuable radical;

    // Extract coefficient and radical from the product
    auto& product = lhs.as<Product>();
    // ...
}
```

This would make the code more maintainable and slightly more efficient.

There's an inconsistency in error handling between the two workflow files:

- `pr-review-comment-reaction.yml` properly handles errors when creating issue reports with a nested try/catch:
```javascript
try {
  const { data: errorIssue } = await github.rest.issues.create({
    // issue creation details
  });
  console.log(`Created error issue: ${errorIssue.html_url}`);
} catch (issueError) {
  console.error(`Failed to create error issue: ${issueError.message}`);
}
```

- `pr-review-reaction.yml` doesn't have this nested error handling:
```javascript
const { data: errorIssue } = await github.rest.issues.create({
  // issue creation details
});
console.log(`Created error issue: ${errorIssue.html_url}`);
```

Recommendation: Add the same nested try/catch error handling to `pr-review-reaction.yml` for consistency and to prevent workflow failures if issue creation fails.

The `compi_lambda` implementation has a limitation that only supports up to 3 variables, but this isn't clearly documented in the Python interface. Consider either:

1. Adding a docstring to explain this limitation
2. Extending the implementation to handle more variables

Currently, users will encounter a runtime error only after trying to use more than 3 variables:
```cpp
else {
    throw std::runtime_error("Unsupported number of variables");
}
```

Code duplication in Python bindings implementation:

The Python bindings implementation contains significant code duplication, particularly in the arithmetic operation methods. For example, there are 6 implementations each of `__add__` and `__mul__` methods with very similar structure.

```cpp
// Example of duplicated code pattern
.def("__add__", +[](const Variable& v, int i) -> Variable {
    std::cout << "Variable + int" << std::endl;
    return Variable(static_cast<const Valuable&>(v) + Valuable(i));
})
.def("__radd__", +[](const Variable& v, int i) -> Variable {
    std::cout << "int + Variable" << std::endl;
    return Variable(Valuable(i) + static_cast<const Valuable&>(v));
})
```

Recommendation: Refactor these methods to reduce duplication by using templates or helper functions. This would improve maintainability and reduce the chance of inconsistencies between similar operations.

