**suggestion:** Extract Excel epoch offset and apply rounding

Define a named constant for the Excel epoch offset instead of using 25567+2 directly, and use Math.round or Math.floor to avoid fractional millisecond errors that could cause date shifts.

```suggestion
const EXCEL_EPOCH_OFFSET = 25567 + 2;
const SECONDS_PER_DAY = 86400;

// Parse dates from Excel cells that use mm/dd/yyyy format
const parseExcelDate = (value) => {
    if (value instanceof Date) {
        return value;
    }
    if (typeof value === 'number') {
        // Convert Excel serialized date to JS date
        const ms = Math.round((value - EXCEL_EPOCH_OFFSET) * SECONDS_PER_DAY * 1000);
        return new Date(ms);
    }
    if (typeof value === 'string') {
        const parts = value.split(/[\/\-]/);
        if (parts.length === 3) {
            const [month, day, year] = parts;
            return new Date(`${year}-${month.padStart(2, '0')}-${day.padStart(2, '0')}`);
        }
    }
    return new Date(value);
};
```

