<issue_to_address>
Consider refactoring the example table extraction logic into a single, declarative pass that validates headers and flattens rows using iterator methods.

Here’s one way to collapse all of that nested looping and repeated error‐handling into a single, declarative pass over your tables. The idea is:

 1. Early-exit if there are no example blocks  
 2. Pull out and validate the “master” header row once  
 3. Ensure every other table’s header matches via `.all(...)`  
 4. `flat_map` every subsequent row into one big `Vec<Vec<String>>`  

```rust
fn extract_examples(scenario: &gherkin::Scenario) 
    -> Result<Option<ExampleTable>, TokenStream>
{
    // 1) skip non-outline or truly empty
    if scenario.keyword != "Scenario Outline" && scenario.examples.is_empty() {
        return Ok(None);
    }

    // 2) get the first table & its rows
    let ex0 = scenario
        .examples
        .get(0)
        .and_then(|ex| ex.table.as_ref())
        .ok_or_else(|| error_to_tokens(&syn::Error::new(
            proc_macro2::Span::call_site(),
            "Scenario Outline missing Examples table",
        )))?;

    let mut rows0 = ex0.rows.clone();
    if rows0.is_empty() {
        return Err(error_to_tokens(&syn::Error::new(
            proc_macro2::Span::call_site(),
            "Examples table must have at least one row",
        )));
    }

    // 3) take master headers and validate every other table matches
    let headers = rows0.remove(0);
    for ex in &scenario.examples[1..] {
        let t = ex.table.as_ref().ok_or_else(|| error_to_tokens(&syn::Error::new(
            proc_macro2::Span::call_site(),
            "Examples table missing rows",
        )))?;
        let other_headers = t.rows.first()
            .ok_or_else(|| error_to_tokens(&syn::Error::new(
                proc_macro2::Span::call_site(),
                "Examples table must have at least one row",
            )))?;
        if *other_headers != headers {
            return Err(error_to_tokens(&syn::Error::new(
                proc_macro2::Span::call_site(),
                "All Examples tables must have the same headers",
            )));
        }
    }

    // 4) flatten all rows (skip header of each block)
    let rows = scenario
        .examples
        .iter()
        .flat_map(|ex| ex.table.as_ref().unwrap().rows.iter().skip(1))
        .cloned()
        .collect();

    Ok(Some(ExampleTable { headers, rows }))
}
```

This approach:

- avoids manual `for row in … enumerate` and inner‐loop length checks  
- uses `.all` and `.flat_map` (plus one explicit clone for the first block)  
- reduces you to only a couple of `error_to_tokens(...)` calls instead of dozens of almost‐identical branches.
</issue_to_address>

