The `handleSelect` function calls `logCorrection`, which is an async operation that can fail (e.g., if `AsyncStorage` fails). The current implementation doesn't handle potential errors from this call, which could lead to an unhandled promise rejection and cause the app to behave unexpectedly.

It's important to wrap async calls in a `try...catch` block to handle errors gracefully. Moving `setShowCorrection(false)` to a `finally` block is also a good practice, as it ensures the panel is always closed, regardless of whether the operation succeeded or failed.

```typescript
  const handleSelect = async (choice: string) => {
    try {
      await logCorrection(choice);
      setStatus('Thanks!');
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Failed to log correction:', error);
      setStatus('Sorry, something went wrong.');
    } finally {
      setShowCorrection(false);
    }
  };
```

